# BEGIN AIDLC CURSOR
# AI-DLC — the committed-vs-ignored split (workspace layout, re-rooted under aidlc/).
# Rule of thumb (vision §5.1): per-user session CURSORS and machine-local
# runtime/derived state are ignored; everything that is the shared work —
# method, registry, state, AUDIT (per-clone shards), artifacts — is committed.
#
# Per-user session cursors — ignored (two teammates legitimately point at
# different spaces/intents at once; committing them would dirty the tree on
# every /aidlc and have teammates fight over the cursor on each switch).
aidlc/active-space
aidlc/.aidlc-active-space-*.tmp
aidlc/spaces/*/intents/active-intent
#
# Machine-local runtime / derived — ignored (regenerated per machine).
# The clone-id token names this clone's audit shard; it MUST stay machine-local
# (gitignored) or every clone from a commit would share a shard and git-conflict.
aidlc/.aidlc-clone-id
# Audit shards from the EPHEMERAL remote runner (Claude Code on the web), whose
# hostname is always `vm`. The per-clone shard model assumes a DURABLE clone: a
# developer's checkout keeps its gitignored clone-id, so it appends to one shard
# forever. An ephemeral container has no such continuity — the id is minted
# fresh on every session, so each session commits a BRAND NEW `vm-<id>.md`
# rather than extending one. That is unbounded file-per-session growth in git,
# and its contents do not pay for it: the remote sessions here are GitHub-issue
# work, so their shards hold only session bookkeeping (HUMAN_TURN /
# SESSION_* / SUBAGENT_COMPLETED) and not one STAGE_*, ARTIFACT_* or SENSOR_*
# event — nothing any reader derives a timing from. Worse, the tree is dirty
# again after every single turn, so a commit-on-dirty workflow produces one
# commit (and one CI run) per turn.
#
# This rule is a default, not a decision made once and forgotten: if a remote
# session ever does run `/aidlc`, `scripts/check-audit-shards.ts` fails the
# quality gate, names the shard and prints the `git add -f` that commits it. An
# ignore rule that could drop real audit history *silently* would not be worth
# having (Codex review on PR #18) — this one cannot.
aidlc/spaces/*/intents/*/audit/vm-*.md
# Cursor's reconstructed subagent identity registry; protected by the adapter
# and recreated per install/session.
aidlc/.aidlc-cursor-subagents/
# Per-conversation session→intent map (resume rebind, P8): per-user runtime
# state keyed by Claude Code session_id, never shared truth.
aidlc/.aidlc-sessions/
aidlc/spaces/*/intents/.aidlc-*
aidlc/spaces/*/intents/*/runtime-graph.json
aidlc/spaces/*/intents/*/.aidlc-*
# Redacted diagnostic reports (/aidlc --doctor --export): regenerated, meant to
# be shared then deleted — machine-local derived output, never shared truth.
aidlc/diagnostics/
#
# COMMITTED (NOT ignored, listed here for the record — do NOT add ignore rules
# for these): aidlc/spaces/*/memory/** · aidlc/spaces/*/codekb/** ·
# aidlc/spaces/*/intents/intents.json · aidlc/spaces/*/intents/*/aidlc-state.md ·
# aidlc/spaces/*/intents/*/audit/*.md (the audit trail, committed as PER-CLONE
# SHARDS: each machine appends to audit/<host>-<clone>.md so git never merges
# concurrent appends — there is intentionally NO .gitattributes merge=union,
# which was proven to corrupt the multi-line audit blocks; the one exception is
# the ephemeral `vm-*` runner, ignored above, whose clone identity never
# survives a session) ·
# aidlc/spaces/*/intents/*/<phase>/<stage>/*.md (artifacts + diary).
# END AIDLC CURSOR

.worktrees/
